}+0{x}_{2}=k-3.$ Therefore, the system has a solution only if\n\n$$\n0=k-3\\,⇒\\,k=3.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120306 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316785 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120306 #edb/ref 1069 "q-34211" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120306 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120306 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine if the vector $u$ lies on the plane $Π:r=sb+tc$, $t,s\\in R$, where  $b=⟨-1,3,5⟩$, $c=⟨1,-5,-7⟩$, and  $u=⟨1,-8,-10⟩.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of $s\\cdot t$ from the decomposition $u=sb+tc.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120306 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3767254e-f5e7-559c-aca3-b6417b0458c5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120306 #edb/ref 1074 "By definition, $u\\in Π$ if there exist $t$ and $s$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sb+tc & =u \\\\ s[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-s+t=1 \\\\ 3s-5t=-8 \\\\ 5s-7t=-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -5 & -8 \\\\ 5 & -7 & -10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -5 \\\\ 5 & -7 & -10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-s+t=1 \\\\ -2t=-5 \\\\ 0=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n-2t=-5\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-s+t & =1 \\\\ -s+\\frac{5}{2} & =1 \\\\ -s & =-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ s & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we found a solution $s=\\frac{3}{2},\\,t=\\frac{5}{2},$ we conclude that $u$ does indeed lie on the plane $Π.$ In fact, we have\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{3}{2}b+\\frac{5}{2}c.\n$$\n\nFinally, $s\\cdot t=\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}=\\frac{15}{4}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120516 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186341680 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120516 #edb/ref 1069 "q-74560" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120516 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120516 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the values of $k$ such that the vector $b$ lies in $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 18\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ -10 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120516 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f53bb678-a419-585c-a65e-c61f25afddb3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120516 #edb/ref 1074 "We know that $b\\in \\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 18\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ -10 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2}=-12 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+7{x}_{2}=-10 \\\\ -6{x}_{1}+18{x}_{2}=k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}-3 & 9 & -12 \\\\ -2 & 7 & -10 \\\\ -6 & 18 & k\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=-\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 7 & -10 \\\\ -6 & 18 & k\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 18 & k\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+6{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k+24\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third row of the augmented matrix now corresponds to the equation $0{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}=k+24.$ Therefore, the system has a solution only if\n\n$$\n0=k+24\\,⇒\\,k=-24.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120676 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120677 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120676 #edb/ref 1061 17469 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120676 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "05c77385-415b-5833-b935-9be3a2038c69" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120676 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121035 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120677 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120750 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120677 #edb/ref 1061 17471 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120677 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "74f8be2d-0f86-52de-8277-fd7db395c80d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120677 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119808 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120678 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120679 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120678 #edb/ref 1061 17477 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120678 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "15713f7b-cd11-55c3-93fd-8040ac62d36f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120678 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121036 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120679 #edb/ref 1061 17478 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120679 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "33abdb88-235d-550f-b5bb-06946454437c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120679 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119809 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120750 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120678 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120750 #edb/ref 1061 23264 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120750 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5cca50af-2a48-5696-bc3a-fc57e95232b0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120750 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119856 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121035 #edb/ref 1064 "Given two vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2,$ the set of all possible linear combinations of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ is\n\n$$\nU = \\left\\{ x_1 \\mathbf{a}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{a}_2 \\: : \\: x_1,x_2\\in\\mathbb{R} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nThe set $U$ is also called the **linear span** of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2,$ and is denoted by $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}.$ We say that $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ are the **generators** of $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}.$\n\nFor example, let's find the linear span generated by the following vectors:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of linear span, we know that if $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ then $\\mathbf{b}$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b} = x_1\\mathbf{a_1} + x_2\\mathbf{a_2}\n$$\n\n for some $x_1,x_2\\in\\mathbb{R}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the linear span of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGeometrically, $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}$ can be viewed as the plane $\\Pi$ that passes through the origin and is parallel to the vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/d5/d5b202475e0bc3ebb07df1f07bab72e9e74b13a99196c2a8a5adb624a7534a03.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121035 #edb/ref 1065 3193 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121035 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c4e2e270-f1fb-5633-9239-4696e8f0a25b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121035 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121036 #edb/ref 1064 "The linear span of vectors can be interpreted geometrically as the points in space that can be reached by adding multiples of those vectors.\n\n- The span of a single nonzero vector is a *line* through the origin. More precisely, if $\\mathbf{a} \\neq \\mathbf{0},$ then $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a} \\}$ determines the *line* with direction vector $\\mathbf{a}$ that passes through the origin. For instance, if\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nthen $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a} \\}$ represents the line $l$ *spanned* by $\\mathbf{a}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/08/080aa6203494e915e28c491a3d86176f7ba634bcbdfe40b094c07d85fb29bb69.png)\n\n- The span of two non-parallel vectors is a *plane* through the origin. More precisely, if $\\mathbf{a} \\not\\parallel \\mathbf{b}$ ($\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are not parallel), then $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\}$ determines the *plane* with direction vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ that passes through the origin. For instance, if\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nthen $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\}$  represents the plane $\\Pi$ *spanned* by $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/56/5606535bd91eb35745a9852ceb451720339105f5a97fb66ccfcb1164ecd55ffd.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121036 #edb/ref 1065 3194 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121036 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c50c93be-7bf2-5511-b456-c60478a53270" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121036 #edb/ref 1067 "Geometric Interpretation of the Linear Span" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238868 #edb/ref 1080 "-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238868 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238868 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5d43760f-5307-568e-a84e-682220ff1a4c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239238 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239238 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239238 #edb/re